## Sodium acetate trihydrate — MIM:0002290

The current owner, [ChEBI 32138](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI:32138), and
the captured MediaDive 1315 name/CAS agree on sodium acetate trihydrate and
CAS 6131-90-4. ChEBI supplies the current formula and structure with three
waters. It relates this compound to sodium acetate by `has part`; the hydrate
is not the same substance as anhydrous sodium acetate. `skos:exactMatch` to the
specific ChEBI term is supported at the owner's defined-substance scope.

The new alias `Na acetate x 3 H2O` comes directly from the exact MediaDive
source name and agrees with its CAS; it is not inferred from the source's
separately discrepant acid-fragment formula. The prior hydrate notations and
ChEBI's `sodium acetate--water (1/3)` remain same-form names. The two role/property
strings in YAML are not names and must not be exported into `other` or used to
approve a role. Existing row 2556 correctly omits them. Its prior support is
withdrawn because the owner changed; genuine final complete-row assessment can
use this chemistry evidence after inspecting the actual generated aliases,
provenance and validation fields.

## Xanthine — MIM:0002647

[ChEBI 17712](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI:17712) represents 9H-xanthine;
[ChEBI 48517](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI:48517) represents 7H-xanthine.
Their primary bodies both assert CAS 69-89-6 and the same normalized mobile-H
structure. Each has a native `is a` relationship to generic xanthine 15318,
and the two are explicitly tautomers. CAS/name/standard-InChI agreement cannot
select a tautomer-pure physical reagent.

The current `MANUAL_CURATION` grade truthfully records the deliberate existing
representation choice under the repository's ingredient-scope profile and
prior chemical-scope decision. It preserves the old CAS lookup as historical
evidence while explicitly denying that it is uniquely resolving. At this
qualified ingredient-representation scope, the retained exact row to 17712 is
scientifically supportable; it does not authorize resolving generic trait or
unqualified source observations to that specific representation. No additional
MIM-to-15318 exact row or global 17712/48517 identity is justified.

All seven owner synonyms match names/label in the fresh 17712 primary body.
The CAS display token can remain a supported scoped registration annotation;
it is not a uniqueness claim. The full generated row must retain the old
qualified source-scope comment and original provenance while stating the
current method honestly. Prior row 2961 support cannot be carried forward by
updating the owner hash. Its two companion claims require current complete
payload review independently of this groundwork.

## Arabinan from Sugar Beet — MIM:0000366

The captured [Megazyme P-ARAB data sheet](https://prod-docs.megazyme.com/documents/Data_Sheet/P-ARAB_DATA.pdf)
identifies arabinan from sugar-beet pulp and gives CAS 11078-27-6. It describes
an arabinose backbone, and [ChEBI 22590](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI:22590)
defines the broader arabinan polysaccharide class. The current `broadMatch`
to arabinan is chemically coherent and preserves botanical specificity.
The former sugar-beet-root parent is not justified for the extracted polymer.
This conclusion does not convert the link into subclass evidence.

The source-preserving `arabinan (sugar beet)` alias is supported. Manufacturer
grade, purity, chain distribution and commercial product identity cannot be
assigned to every source occurrence or to the generic owner merely from the
reference product. The CAS is documented on that reference product; its
registration alone does not prove sugar-beet-specific object scope or an
exact commercial product identity. This bounded review has not exhaustively
established CAS scope and does not approve the CAS exact row by itself.

Rows 412–414 remain WITHHOLD in the existing review. Their inherited reason
describes the older FOODON parent, whereas current row 412 already targets
CHEBI:22590. Preserve that historical negative finding and perform explicit
new relation/registry/full-row assessments against actual generated bytes;
neither fixing the target nor the separate four-source identity review clears
the prior three-row release hold automatically. Retain the local registry
handle as the distinct material identity if later independently supported.

## Cerium(III) nitrate hexahydrate — MIM:0002742

The captured parenthesized MediaDive 1587 name/CAS, PubChem CID16211466 and
independent Sigma 238538 primary product content converge on Ce(III) nitrate
hexahydrate, CAS 10294-41-4, formula CeH12N3O15 and six waters. The malformed
flattened producer label remains `REJECTED_LABEL`; only the verified
parenthesized spelling can act as a resolving synonym. No supplier grade,
recipe quantity, biological role or source-wide export identity follows.

Fresh [NLM M0319657](https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/M0319657.json) gives the stored
label and explicitly types this resource as `Concept`. The broader Ce(III)
nitrate link is a hydrate-to-parent `broadMatch`, not exact identity or
subclass evidence. The current note's word `descriptor` is still wrong:
issue #1367 remains a current, applicable owner-evidence wording defect.
Its minor severity does not erase it or provide an export-gate exception.

At the science-claim level, the hexahydrate identity/CAS and broader Ce(III)
nitrate relation have independent positive evidence; neither depends on the
resource being a Descriptor rather than a Concept. Whether a specific future
installation payload asserts, propagates or materially relies on that wrong
word is still unassessed. That requires all actual generated/proposed/before
cells and the exact selection target. A missing literal word alone will not
resolve applicability. No current canonical Cerium row was present in the
inspected table; no future one is invented here.

